/-!
# A surjective étale cover is dominated by a finite flat cover, Zariski-locally

This file proves Stacks 00UG: an étale ring map `R → S` which is surjective on spectra is
dominated by a single finite flat cover `R → S'`, Zariski-locally on `Spec(S')`.

The proof is the source's. Every prime `q` of `S` has a `g ∉ q` with `R → S_g` standard étale
(Mathlib's `Algebra.IsEtaleAt.exists_isStandardEtale`, the finitely presented case of Stacks
00UE, which applies since `Algebra.Etale` contains `Algebra.FinitePresentation`), so the set of
such `g` generates the unit ideal of `S`; quasi-compactness of `Spec(S)` (Stacks 00E8) is the
step extracting a finite subset `t` still generating the unit ideal. Lemma 00UF applied to each
`R → S_g`, `g ∈ t`, produces a finite flat cover `R → S'_g`, and `S' = ⨂_{g ∈ t} S'_g` — built
here as an iterated binary tensor product along an induction on `t` — is the cover sought: each
`S'_g` maps into it, and for a prime `q'` of `S'` one chooses `g` after `q'`, namely a `g ∈ t`
with `g ∉ q` for some prime `q` of `S` above `q' ∩ R`, which exists because `Spec(S) → Spec(R)`
is surjective.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Etale.ZariskiFiniteFlatCover.solution`: Stacks 00UG.

## Implementation notes

The factorization clause is carried through the induction in the form supplied by 00UF — a prime
of `S_g` and a prime of `S'` lying over a common prime of `R`, and a factorization through the
canonical localization `Localization.Away g'` — and only at the very end transported to an
arbitrary localization of `S'` away from `g'`. Both transports, the one along an `R`-algebra map
`S'_g → S'` in the induction step and the one along the identity of `S'` at the end, are the same
lemma, `exists_comp_eq_of_algHom`: a factorization through `Localization.Away g''` and an
`R`-algebra map `ψ` sending `g''` to `g'` give a factorization through any localization away from
`g'`, by `IsLocalization.lift`.

The source reduces to the case `S = ∏_{i} S_i` with each `R → S_i` standard étale, and then
tensors the covers of the `S_i` together. That reduction is not carried out here: the finite
cover `t` of `Spec(S)` by standard étale localizations is kept as it is and the covers Stacks
00UF supplies for its members are tensored together directly, which is the same tensor product
and one construction fewer. Consequently the factorization produced is through `S_g` for a
`g ∈ t`, that is through `S` itself, as the conclusion asks.

The comap clause `φ⁻¹(q'S'_{g'}) = q` of 00UF is not needed and is dropped as soon as 00UF is
used; the conclusion of 00UG constrains no prime of `S`.

## References

* [StacksProject 00UG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00UG), Lemma 10.144.6.
* [StacksProject 00UF](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00UF), the standard étale case.
* [StacksProject 00UE](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00UE), an étale ring map is Zariski
  locally standard étale.
